digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N10" [label="(16,2,0) The additional restoring force from pinning contact stiffens the gas-water interface."];
  "N11" [label="(16,3,0) The additional restoring force from pinning contact speeds up wave propagation."];
  "N12" [label="(0,0,0) Source: (10,3,21,0) The additional restoring force is exerted by the pinned contact line on the deformed surface."];
  "N13" [label="(17,1,0) The effect of additional restoring force is intensified when wall spacing is narrowed."];
  "N14" [label="(17,2,0) The effect of additional restoring force is intensified when the water interface becomes curved."];
  "N15" [label="(19,2,0) The in-depth knowledge of water waves shares a common determinant: assuming the air or gas phase above the water interface is boundary-free and inviscid."];
  "N2" [label="(7,1,0) These works contributed to describing a wavenumber-to-frequency relation (dispersion relation)."];
  "N21" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Pinning contact creates additional restoring force that stiffens interface and speeds up waves, and this effect intensifies with narrower spacing and curved interfaces, therefore pinning constraints create unique wave signatures."];
  "N22" [label="(0,0,0) If pinning constraints modify wave properties and the effect intensifies with geometry changes, then pinning-induced wave behavior reflects interface geometry."];
  "N24" [label="(0,0,0) If extensive studies develop theoretical models for multiple wave configurations, then a mature theoretical foundation exists for those configurations."];
  "N26" [label="(0,0,0) If all existing studies share a common assumption, and configurations violating that assumption lack studies, then those configurations represent research gaps."];
  "N33" [label="(26,1,0) The water-repellent feature can be compromised by spontaneous changes in the environment."];
  "N34" [label="(27,1,0) The plastron can experience depletion due to gas dissolution."];
  "N35" [label="(27,2,0) The plastron can experience depletion due to pressure-induced impalement."];
  "N36" [label="(28,1,0) The plastron could eventually be lost, resulting in a fully wetted surface (Wenzel state)."];
  "N37" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Plastron can be depleted by gas dissolution, pressure, and environmental changes leading to Wenzel state, therefore plastrons are inherently unstable and require monitoring."];
  "N38" [label="(0,0,0) If a system is subject to multiple failure mechanisms that compromise its function, then monitoring that system's condition is necessary."];
  "N39" [label="(29,1,0) The plastron condition is typically assessed optically by bright-field microscopy."];
  "N40" [label="(29,2,0) The plastron condition is typically assessed optically by total internal reflection technique."];
  "N41" [label="(29,3,0) The plastron condition is typically assessed optically by confocal microscopy."];
  "N42" [label="(30,1,0) Acoustics-based alternatives to optics have limitations due to complexity and cost of integrating a transducer."];
  "N43" [label="(30,2,0) Acoustics-based alternatives to optics have limitations due to lack of access to plastron dynamics and intermediate wetting states."];
  "N44" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Optical methods exist but acoustic alternatives have significant limitations in complexity and accessing dynamics, therefore current monitoring approaches have practical constraints."];
  "N45" [label="(0,0,0) If existing monitoring methods each have significant limitations, then improved monitoring approaches are needed."];
  "N52" [label="(0,0,0) If mechanical waves carry properties of their propagation medium, and X is a propagation medium, then mechanical waves can carry properties of X."];
  "N54" [label="(32,1,0) Examining dynamics of gas-water interface constrained by microstructures in response to mechanical stress could provide means for remotely monitoring plastron's condition."];
  "N58" [label="(0,0,0) If capillary waves propagate on liquid-gas interfaces, and X has a liquid-gas interface, then capillary waves can propagate on X."];
  "N59" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Pinning constraints create unique wave signatures reflecting interface geometry, and plastron has pinned contact lines at microstructures, therefore capillary waves on plastron will exhibit behavior reflecting plastron state."];
  "N60" [label="(0,0,0) If pinning constraints create wave signatures reflecting interface geometry, and a system has pinned contact lines, then waves on that system exhibit behavior reflecting system state."];
  "N61" [label="(33,1,0) This work presents an original configuration of capillary waves induced by ultrasound."];
  "N62" [label="(33,2,0) The capillary waves travel on a gas-water interface bounded to solid microstructures of a superhydrophobic plastron."];
  "N67" [label="(0,0,0) Need to study capillary waves on microstructure-constrained plastron interfaces with monitoring capability."];
  "N9" [label="(16,1,0) The pinning contact of the water interface to channel walls can exert an additional restoring force opposite to interface displacement."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes to study ultrasound-driven capillary waves propagating along gas-water interfaces pinned to microstructured superhydrophobic plastrons, varying contact-line density and pillar height to determine how microstructure controls wave dispersion and to assess whether those waves can serve as a remote, non-optical indicator of plastron condition and intermediate wetting states.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a research gap concerns microstructure-constrained capillary waves and a monitoring need concerns plastron condition, an ultrasound-driven capillary-wave study on superhydrophobic plastrons can jointly investigate the wave physics and evaluate sensing potential."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Because established water-wave knowledge commonly assumes a boundary-free gas phase while systematic studies of capillary waves pinned to microstructure arrays are still lacking, microstructure-pinned capillary waves constitute a research gap."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Examining mechanically driven dynamics of the microstructure-constrained gas-water interface is a plausible strategy for remotely monitoring plastron condition."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) An original configuration of ultrasound-induced capillary waves is a plausible experimental approach for studying microstructure-constrained plastron interfaces with monitoring potential."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Because plastrons require monitoring and capillary waves on plastrons can reflect interface state, capillary-wave analysis is a plausible basis for plastron monitoring."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Prior studies provide theoretical descriptions of capillary-wave dispersion and propagation speed for known interfacial-wave configurations."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Because mechanical waves carry properties of their propagation medium, capillary waves traveling on the superhydrophobic plastron's gas-water interface can carry properties of that interface."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Given practical constraints in current plastron monitoring methods and the capacity of waves on a plastron interface to carry interface properties, mechanical waves are a plausible route for improved plastron monitoring."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Capillary waves can travel on the gas-water interface bounded to the solid microstructures of a superhydrophobic plastron."];
